From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EglantinePS5
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] util/mesa_cache_db: flush policy switches, writes outside the part's lock, held-back gets from memory; vk_pipeline_cache: the app's change count

T175d. After about 250-300 s of the before-play pass the console's /data disk
goes slow: every flush of a fast database (lead-mesh-32: two write calls, the
.db bytes then the batch of index lines, about 9 KiB, made under the part's
lock every 64 entries) took 190-960 ms, and gets on the compile threads waited
for that lock (17-770 ms). No fsync, O_SYNC or flock is made per flush or get
(owned databases keep their files open and locked from open to close).

1. The flush policy, read once per process (unset: as before, 64 entries or
   1 MiB): RADV_PS5_CACHE_FLUSH_MIB=<1-256>, RADV_PS5_CACHE_FLUSH_N=<entries,
   0 none; with _MIB set and _N unset: none>, RADV_PS5_CACHE_FLUSH_S=<s> (at a
   put, once the oldest waiting entry is that old). The batch of index lines
   grows past 64 when the policy lets it.

2. RADV_PS5_CACHE_MEM_PENDING=1: held-back .db bytes are kept in memory of
   the part's own (not the stdio buffer). A flush moves the batch to in-flight
   buffers, lets go of the lock, writes the bytes and then the index lines with
   pwrite at their known offsets, and takes the lock again. One flush per part
   is in flight; puts meanwhile start a new batch (a batch of 4 flushes' worth,
   8 MiB at least, waits for the one in flight). A get of a held-back or
   in-flight entry is copied from memory (before: it wrote the batch out under
   the lock first); a get from the files is a pread, which no flush holds up.
   Compaction, entry removal, zap and close wait for the flush in flight and
   write the rest under the lock, through streams opened afresh. Access times
   are written at compaction and close only (before: every 256 hits, one write
   call per entry under the lock).

3. The RADV_PS5_CACHE line gets flush_kib, flush_max_ms (longest flush in the
   10 s), get_max_ms (longest disk_cache_get in the 10 s), mem_hits and
   flush_wait_ms (puts waiting for a flush in flight), and the fast on line the
   policy when a switch changed it.

4. vk_pipeline_cache: a count of objects added to or removed from a
   pipeline cache the app holds a handle to (the objects read from
   vkCreatePipelineCache's initial data are not counted: the handle is made
   after them; nor is a looked-up raw entry turned into the object its own
   bytes describe, which every boot pipeline does), offered through eden_radv_pipeline_cache_changes (weak, as
   lead-mesh-41's hooks). EglantinePS5's EDEN_UP_DRIVER_CACHE_SKIP_SAME keeps
   vulkan_pipelines.bin as it is while it is 0.

Crash safety is as lead-mesh-39 made it: data is written before the index
lines that point at it; at the next open a torn index line, index lines past
the data and data no line points at are cut. A kill loses the held-back batch
(compiled again later). The files' format, names and the cache UUID do not
change; an existing database is read as before. Ours.

Numbered lead-mesh-44 in T176: written as lead-mesh-43 on T173, where
T175b's lead-mesh-43 (long-lived lookup outside) took that number. Applies
after lead-mesh-41, -42 and -43.
---
